      "A. For all the convenience afforded by the internet, each of the activities you perform online comes with a small cost. A few grams of carbon dioxide are emitted due to the energy needed to run your devices and power the wireless networks you access. Less obvious, but perhaps even more energy intensive, are the data centres and vast servers needed to support the internet and store the content we access over it.",
      "B. Although the energy needed for a single internet search or email is small, 53.6% of the global population now use the internet. The carbon footprint of our gadgets, the internet, and the systems supporting them account for about 3.7% of global greenhouse emissions. If we were to divide the 1.7 billion tonnes of greenhouse gas emissions estimated to be produced in the manufacture and running of digital technologies between all internet users around the world, it means each of us is responsible for 414kg of carbon dioxide a year.",
      "C. Calculating digital carbon footprints is more complex than it appears because few people can agree on what should and should not be included. This disagreement arises because some argue that emissions from manufacturing computing hardware should be counted, while others believe they should not. Additionally, the inclusion of emissions from the staff and buildings of technology companies is debated, causing further complexity. The figures around the running of data centres are also disputed; many run on renewable energy, which reduces their carbon footprint, while some companies buy “carbon offsets” to mitigate their energy use, leading to differing opinions on their actual impact.",
